## ABSTRACT
The rational reticular design of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) from building units of known geometries is essential for enriching the diversity of MOF structures. Unexpected and intriguing structures, however, can also arise from subtle changes in the rigidity/length of organic linkers and/or synthetic conditions. Herein, we report three uranium-based MOF structures—i.e., NU-135X \((X=0, 1, 2)\)—synthesized from trigonal planar uranyl nodes and triptycene-based hexacarboxylate ligands with variable arm lengths. A new chiral 3,6-connected nuc net was observed in NU-1350, while the extended versions of the ligand led to 3-fold catenated MOFs (NU-1351 and NU-1352) with rare 3,6-connected cml-c3 nets. The differences in the topology of NU-1350 and NU-1351/NU-1352 could be attributed to the slight distortions of the shorter linker in the former from the ideal trigonal prism geometry to an octahedral geometry when coordinated to the trigonal planar uranyl nodes.
## KEYWORDS
reticular chemistry, uranium-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), hexacarboxylate, 3,6-connected net, catenation, actinide
## 1 Introduction
Reticular chemistry [1, 2] is often applied to the rational design and syntheses of 3-periodic ordered framework structures including metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). Periodic nets, particularly those with minimal transitivity, are vital for the practical application of reticular chemistry to the structures of the resulted frameworks [3–8]. The reticular chemistry structure resource (RCSR) [9] is an important database of known nets and an invaluable toolbox for the design of 3-periodic crystalline frameworks, such as MOFs.
MOFs are a class of crystalline materials synthesized from organic ligands and inorganic nodes. Many applications including, but not limited to, catalysis [10], water capture [11], gas storage and separation [12–14], have been explored on account of their high surface areas, designability, tailorable porosity and pore sizes. The deliberately designed synthesis of MOFs is attractive because the preselected organic and inorganic building units can be assembled into the targeted network with atomic precision. Nonetheless, many intriguing nets have also been discovered serendipitously from simple reactions between two building units. For example, the discovery of 3,6-connected qom net—qom denotes queen of MOFs—was serendipitous from MOF-177 [15] synthesized from 6-connected \(Z_{\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}}\) cluster and 3-connected 1,3,5-benzenetribenzoate (BTB). The
non-catenated qom net is of high interest for the construction of highly porous isoreticular MOFs such as MOF-180 and MOF-200 [16].
Many novel topological networks and secondary building units (SBUs) have been continuously unveiled [17–26] in actinide-based MOFs. For example, we have reported a uranium organic framework [27]—NU-1301—from simple tritopic linkers and trigonal planar uranium-based nodes, \(\left[\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}\right]^{-}\). This MOF was based on a 3-connected nun net with a transitivity of [17, 18, 11, 8]—nun denotes Northwestern University net. The fact that nun net has 17 types of nodes and 18 types of edges makes it highly challenging to design deliberately such a structure based on the original nun net. From the point of view of organic linker design, we have recently used rigid hexacarboxylate triptycenes with ideal \(D_{3h}\) symmetry to access targeted MOFs based on a 6-connected acs net or a 6,12-connected alb net [28, 29]. Thus, it would be interesting to explore the outcome in relation to MOF structures from the combination of hexacarboxylate linkers with rigid trigonal planar uranyl nodes while there is no single default resultant network.
Here we report three uranium-based MOFs made from triptycene-based hexacarboxylate ligands and trigonal planar uranyl nodes, \(\left[\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}\right]^{-}\). NU-1350 has been synthesized from the combination of peripherally extended triptycene
\(\left(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}-1\right)\) and uranyl salts, and topological analysis reveals NU-1350 is based on a new 3,6-connected nuc net—nuc denotes Northwestern University Chemistry, which was added to the RCSR database. The nuc net, with a transitivity of [23], is a uniform net based on triangles and octahedra. Interestingly, 3-fold catenated MOF structures (NU-1351 and NU-1352) were obtained from the assembly of triangular \(\left[\mathrm{UO}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}\right]^{-}\)nodes and the extended version of ligands—\(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}-2\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}-3\)—respectively. These two MOFs are based on a rare 3,6-connected cml-c3 net.
## 2 Results and discussion
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow, showing (Fig. S4 in the ESM) that the framework of NU-1350 decomposes at about \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Structural and topological analysis [31] of NU-1350 revealed that the uranyl nodes, as the triangular SBUs, are linked to the hexacarboxylate ligands (PET-1) to assemble a new 3-periodic uranium-based MOF exhibiting an unprecedented nuc net with a transitivity of [23]. It is worth noting that the coordination mode of the uranyl node is hexagonal bipyramid where a uranyl ion is coordinated by three carboxylates from PET-1 via a chelating bidentate mode. When considering the carbon of the carboxylate as a point of extension in the structure, the uranyl node can be viewed as a triangular SBU. The nuc net, composed of two types of nodes, and three types of edges, is a chiral and uniform net constructed from triangles and octahedra. Interestingly, PET-1, previously found to be a rigid linker with a trigonal prismatic shape in both hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks [32] (HOFs) and MOFs such as NU-1500 and NU-1600 [28, 29], can be viewed (Fig. 2) as an octahedral SBU in the structure of NU-1350. Coordination to the trigonal planar uranyl nodes forces the PET-1 to twist with dihedral angles of \(10.9^{\circ}\) and \(20.3^{\circ}\) (Fig. S9 in the ESM), and thus the points of extension of the 6-connected PET-1 match the vertex figure of an octahedron.
Alternatively, the PET-1 ligand can also be considered as a tertiary building unit which can consist of two types of interconnected 3-connected triangular SBUs [3, 4, 33, 34]. Topological analysis reveals the topology of NU-1350 can also be described as the 3-connected srs net. The srs net is a self-dual, uniform and regular net with a transitivity of [1111] [35].
PET-2—an extended version of the PET-1 ligand—and uranyl nitrate under similar conditions were used to make NU-1350 yielding block-like yellow crystals of NU-1351 (Fig. 3). The SCXRD analysis (Table S1 in the ESM) revealed that NU-1351 crystallizes in the orthorhombic space group \(Cmc2_{1}\) \((a=24.9231(8)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\), \(b=53.340(2)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) and \(c=15.5302(3)\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) at \(100\,\mathrm{K}\)). NU-1351 has a similar formula \(\left(\mathrm{DMA}\right)_{2}\left[\left(\mathrm{UO}_{2}\right)_{2}\left(\mathrm{PET}-2\right)\right]\) to NU-1350, with DMA as charge-balancing cations. Similar to NU-1350, each uranyl node in NU-1351 is also coordinated to three PET-2 ligands and each PET-2 ligand is linked to six different inorganic building units. In contrast to the octahedral geometry of PET-1 in NU-1350, the PET-2 ligand in NU-1351 has a trigonal prismatic shape with dihedral angles of the linker arms
close to \(0^{\circ}\) (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Therefore, PET-2 in NU-1351, when compared to PET-1 in NU-1350, experiences less strain when coordinated to the trigonal planar uranyl nodes. Topological analysis after optimization in Systre (Fig. S11 in the ESM) revealed that NU-1351 is based on a 3-fold catenated 3,6-connected cml net (cml-c3). It is worth noting that the cml net with a transitivity of [23], was observed previously in neodymium- based MOFs [36].
The use of a longer peripherally extended triptycene ligand (PET-3) [29, 37] with uranyl nodes resulted (Fig. 4) in the formation of NU-1352. The SCXRD analysis (Table S1 in the ESM) revealed that NU-1352, with a formula of \((\mathrm{DMA})_{2}[(\mathrm{UO}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{PET}-3)]\), crystallizes in the orthorhombic space group \(Cmc2_{1}\), similar to those of NU-1352. PXRD patterns support (Figs. S2 and S3 in the ESM) the phase purity of the bulk NU-1351 and NU-1352. TGA experiments under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow revealed (Figs. S5 and S6 in the ESM) that NU-1351 and NU-1352, in common with NU-1350, decompose at \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Additionally, NU-1352 has a 3-fold catenated framework, similar to that of NU-1351 and is
based on the cml-c3 net. We surmised that PET ligands, with longer and more flexible arms that can accommodate triangular uranyl nodes in MOF lattices, prefer to form the catenated cml net, wherein the geometry of 6-connected nodes is close to ideal trigonal prisms. In the case of NU-1350, the distortions of the shorter linker—PET-1—result in an octahedral geometry deviating from the ideal trigonal prismatic geometry. The unprecedented nuc net observed in NU-1350 can be attributed to the distortions of organic ligands when coordinated to trigonal planar uranyl nodes [38–40].
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we have synthesized three new uranium-based MOFs from hexacarboxylate linkers. NU-1350 is based on the newly discovered 3,6-connected nuc net while NU-1351 are NU-1352 are 3-fold catenated MOFs with 3,6-connected cml-c3 nets. These examples represent a new family of uranium-organic crystalline frameworks. Also, a new uniform and chiral net—nuc—was observed during our exploration, and we postulate that hexacarboxylate ligands [5, 41] with combined rigidity and flexibility could potentially be used to obtain nuc-MOFs based on triangular uranyl nodes. We envision that additional attractive actinide-based MOF structures will be studied and reported in the near future on account of their distinctive coordination environments.
